Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and how they will be used  
(only the columns that really matter for building the pick list for order 423)

orders.id     : id of the order we have to pick (423)  
orderlines.order_id : link every line back to the order (423)  
orderlines.product_id : which products are needed  
orderlines.qty   : quantity requested per line (sequence must be respected)  

inventory.product_id : connects inventory to the ordered product  
inventory.location_id : tells where the stock is stored  
inventory.qty    : quantity that can still be picked  
inventory.purchase_id : lets us find when that stock was bought (FIFO rule)  

purchases.id    : joins to inventory.purchase_id  
purchases.purchased : date of purchase – older first (FIFO)  

locations.id     : joins to inventory.location_id  
locations.warehouse : must be = 1 (only pick from warehouse 1)  
locations.aisle   : aisle to show on the picking list  
locations.position  : position (slot) inside the aisle  

Typical WHERE / JOIN conditions that will be used  
• orders.id = 423  
• orderlines.order_id = orders.id  
• inventory.product_id = orderlines.product_id  
• inventory.purchase_id = purchases.id  
• inventory.location_id = locations.id  
• locations.warehouse = 1  
• inventory.qty > 0  

Below are ten exploratory SQLite queries (LIMIT 20) that go from very simple
inspection to an almost‑complete pick‑allocation query.  
All names are wrapped in double quotes to stay on the safe side.

```sql
-- 1. Show every order‑line that belongs to order 423
SELECT *
FROM "orderlines"
WHERE "order_id" = 423
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Aggregate the total quantity needed for each product in order 423
SELECT "product_id",
       SUM("qty") AS "total_order_qty"
FROM "orderlines"
WHERE "order_id" = 423
GROUP BY "product_id"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. See raw inventory rows for the products in order 423 (all warehouses)
SELECT *
FROM "inventory"
WHERE "product_id" IN (
      SELECT "product_id"
      FROM "orderlines"
